  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1015 PM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nFLASH FLOODING POSSIBLE MONDAY:\n\nWe're still seeing isolated convection ongoing across the \nregion early tonight, and CAM's are in unison indicating \nstratiform-type rainfall developing across portions of the CWA \nthroughout the remainder of the night. Some of this, as seen in \nnortheastern Utah and creeping into northwestern Colorado, is \nbeing driven by a cold front. Without daytime heating to further\nsupport the active weather, nighttime impacts should be \nminimal, but don't be surprised if we get a stronger cell or two\nover the next few hours. Otherwise, the front looks to cool the\narea back to near-normal for the next week.\n\nThese overnight storms and cooling associated with the passing \ncold front may marginally stabilize the atmosphere. To add to \nthe stabilization, CAM's are in good agreement about broken \ncloud cover within the southeast quadrant of the CWA Monday\nmorning. However, some guidance's cloud cover looks marginally \nless dense than it has over the past couple days, and the \nnorthwestern half of the CWA is still on track to see relatively\nclear skies in the morning. Plus, we are still slated to sit\nunderneath a favorable region of the upper-level jet streak. So,\nwhile it may be struggle for some areas to garner significant \ninstability, there is evidence that there will be a handful of \npowerful storms capable of localized heavy rainfall, gusty \nwinds, and some hail. Most hail will be sub-severe, but we've \nseen a couple supercells capable of large hail over the past 48 \nhours, so don't rule out a severe storm or two.\n\nCAM's are hinting at the best area for strong storm development\nto come on the edge of the densest cloud cover where \ndestabilization will be easier to achieve and moisture will be \npotent. This corridor looks to span from southeastern Utah \ntowards the Grand Mesa, though the exact location will vary \ndepending on said morning cloud cover. As the event unfolds \nMonday and active weather transits eastwards, the expectation is\nfor storms to stratiform out given the likely more stable \nenvironment over the San Juans and central Colorado mountains. \nEven so, embedded stronger cells will be possible. For those \nstronger cells, localized heavy rainfall will raise concerns of \nflash flooding, especially over vulnerable areas and burn scars.\nIn the scenario that storms struggle to organize or cloud cover\nspans a greater area than anticipated, stratiform rainfall will\nbe favored region-wide. As mentioned before, embedded stronger \ncells could and likely will still develop, which means a flash \nflooding threat will exist regardless of storm mode. As always,\nbe weather aware Monday (and beyond) and stay up-to-date with \nadditional information as it becomes available.\n\nACTIVE WEATHER EXPECTED THROUGH THE END OF THE WEEK:\n\nThe CWA remains in southwest flow aloft as the first low tracks\nover the Midwest and another low drops into the PacNW. This \nwill ultimately keep moisture in place through at least the mid-\nweek, so expect the chances for showers and storms to continue.\nOn Wednesday the models are hinting that a subtle shortwave \nmoves in from the southwest. If this were to happen it would \ncertainly be a focus for showers and storms especially given the\npersistent moisture pool. On Friday, moisture begins to \ndiminish as the low moves eastwards through the western US.",
